course: everyday
lesson: 74
title: Flossing and caring for your teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A toothbrush cleans the fronts, backs and tops of the teeth, but it cannot reach the narrow gaps between them, so once a day we clean there with floss or a small brush made for the gaps.
explain: Teeth are strong but not made for everything: opening packets, chewing pencils or crunching ice can chip or crack them, and fizzy or sugary drinks every day cause decay.
explain: When something goes wrong, act calmly: a bleeding gap from a baby tooth stops with gentle pressure, but a knocked-out grown-up tooth needs a dentist at once.
explain: A boy in Adelaide gets a front tooth knocked out at football; his coach picks it up by the white top, puts it in a cup of milk and drives him straight to the dentist, who puts it back.
fact: A toothbrush cannot reach between the teeth, so floss or an interdental brush is used once a day.
fact: Floss is slid gently between the teeth and curved around each tooth, because snapping it down hurts the gums.
fact: Sharp things like forks and pins cut the gums and can chip teeth, so they are never used to pick the teeth.
fact: Sugary and fizzy drinks cause tooth decay, so water and milk are the best everyday drinks.
fact: When a baby tooth comes out and the gap bleeds, biting on clean gauze (a soft cloth pad) or tissue for a few minutes stops it.
fact: A knocked-out grown-up tooth is held by the white top (the crown), not the root, kept in milk and taken to a dentist at once.
fact: A grown-up tooth can sometimes be saved if it is put back quickly.
fact: A tooth that hurts with cold or sweet things may have a hole that a dentist needs to fix.
fact: Opening packets with your teeth and chewing pencils or ice can crack teeth.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum is not digested, but it passes through the body in a few days; it does not stay for seven years.
fact: Breath smells in the morning because germs grow in the mouth overnight when there is less spit.
exercise: Why do we use floss or an interdental brush? | A toothbrush cannot reach between the teeth | Floss makes the teeth much whiter than brushing ever can | Floss is only for people with braces | It makes toothpaste work faster
exercise: How should floss be used? | Slid gently between the teeth and curved around each tooth | Snapped down hard so it gets right under the gums to the root | Pulled quickly back and forth on the gums | Wrapped around all the teeth at once
exercise: A bit of meat is stuck between your teeth. What should you use? | Floss or an interdental brush | The sharp point of a fork | A pin or a needle | Your fingernail pushed in hard
exercise: Why are fizzy drinks every day bad for teeth? | The sugar and acid cause tooth decay | The bubbles pop on the teeth and crack the enamel | They make the teeth too cold | They wash away all the spit
exercise: What is the best everyday drink for your teeth? | Water or milk | Cola with lots of ice | Sweet fruit squash | Cans of energy drinks
exercise: Your baby tooth falls out and the gap bleeds. What should you do? | Bite gently on clean gauze or a tissue for a few minutes | Rinse hard with water again and again until the blood is gone | Poke the gap with your tongue to stop it | Go to the hospital straight away
exercise: A friend's grown-up tooth is knocked out. How should it be held? | By the white top, not the root | By the root, so the top stays clean | Wrapped tightly in a dry tissue | Wiped clean with a towel first of all
exercise: Where should a knocked-out grown-up tooth be kept on the way to the dentist? | In a little milk | In a dry paper bag | In a glass of fizzy water | In the freezer to keep cold
exercise: Why must a knocked-out grown-up tooth reach the dentist quickly? | It can sometimes be put back and saved | Otherwise it slowly turns back into a baby tooth | The dentist wants to keep it | A new one grows faster that way
exercise: Your tooth hurts when you eat ice cream. What should you do? | Tell a parent so you can see a dentist | Chew only on that side to harden it | Ignore it, it will stop by itself | Eat more cold things to get used to it
exercise: How should you open a tough crisp packet? | Use scissors or ask for help | Tear it with your front teeth | Bite the corner off really hard | Hold it in your teeth and pull
exercise: Why should you not chew pencils or crunch ice? | Hard things that are not food can crack teeth | They make your teeth too clean | They are good exercise for the jaw, so you should do it more | They only harm baby teeth
exercise: You swallowed your chewing gum by mistake. What happens? | It passes through your body in a few days | It stays stuck in your stomach for seven years | It sticks your stomach together | It dissolves into sugar at once
exercise: Why does breath often smell bad in the morning? | Germs grow overnight when there is less spit | Teeth rot a little bit more every single night | Dreams make the breath smell | The pillow puts germs in the mouth
test: A girl in Phoenix brushes well twice a day but never cleans between her teeth. What could happen? | Plaque between the teeth could cause decay there | Nothing, because brushing well reaches everywhere in the mouth | Her teeth would grow crooked | Her teeth would get too clean
test: Which is true about chewing gum? | Swallowed gum passes through, though it is not digested | Swallowed gum stays inside the stomach for seven whole years | Gum is digested like bread | Gum is healthy to swallow every day
test: A boy in Cork knocks out a front grown-up tooth on the trampoline. What should the adult do? | Hold it by the top, put it in milk and go to a dentist at once | Throw it away, a new one will grow | Scrub the root clean with a toothbrush and soap, then let it dry in the air | Wait a week to see if the gap heals
test: A boy uses a sharp pin to get popcorn out of his teeth. What is the danger? | He could cut his gums or chip a tooth | The pin could make the popcorn stick harder | Nothing, a pin is the best tool | The pin would make his teeth loose for ever
test: A family drinks cola with every meal. What would a dentist probably say? | Choose water or milk most of the time | Cola is fine if you drink it with a straw | Cola makes teeth stronger | Drink juice instead, it has no sugar
test: Why would a dentist tell you not to snap floss down hard? | It hurts and can damage the gums | It breaks the floss much too quickly | It makes the teeth loose | It puts germs on the floss
test: What would happen if you pressed a clean tissue on a bleeding baby tooth gap for a few minutes? | The small bleeding would usually stop | The bleeding would get worse and worse | A new tooth would grow at once | The gap would become infected from the tissue
test: A girl uses her teeth as scissors to open everything. What is the risk? | Her teeth could chip or crack | Her teeth would become sharper | Her jaw would grow much too big | Her teeth would get much cleaner
test: What does it mean if a tooth hurts with sweet foods? | It may have a hole that needs a dentist | It is growing a brand new root underneath | The tooth is just very healthy | Sweet foods always hurt teeth
test: bit of chicken stuck tween my teeth, gonna use a fork | No, use floss, sharp things cut gums and chip teeth | Yes, a fork is the quickest way | Yes, use a pin if the fork is too big | Yes, just be quick about it
test: my mate swallowed his gum, he says itll stay in him 7 yrs | No, it passes through the body in a few days | Yes, gum stays inside for seven years, everyone knows | Yes, it sticks to the stomach forever | No, it stays for about one year
test: knocked his big tooth out at footy!! where do i hold it | By the white top, keep it in milk and see a dentist now | By the root, wash it with soap | Wrap it in tissue, see a dentist next week | Throw it away, a new one grows
test: can i have a fizzy drink every day with lunch | Water or milk is better, fizzy drinks cause decay | Yes, if you drink it through a straw | Yes, fizzy drinks clean your teeth with all the bubbles | Yes, if it is a small can
explain: A boy in Ohio never flossed; food stuck between two teeth for months, and a hole grew there where the brush could not reach, which the dentist had to fill.
explain: A girl in Wales chewed ice and opened bottles with her teeth; one day a front tooth cracked, and she needed several visits to fix it.
fact: About a third of each tooth's surface is between the teeth, where a brush cannot reach without floss.
fact: Using teeth as tools, such as opening packets or bottles, is a common way children crack and chip teeth.
test: gums bleed a bit when i brush, should i stop brushing there | No, keep brushing gently and floss, bleeding gums need cleaning | Yes, stop brushing there for good | Yes, rub salt hard on them | Only brush there on Sundays
test: bro opens bottles with his teeth like a legend lol | It can crack a tooth, use an opener | It makes your teeth stronger over time | It is fine if the cap is already loose | It only ever hurts the plastic bottles
test: whats the point of floss, i brush twice a day | It cleans between the teeth where holes start and a brush cannot reach | It makes your teeth whiter only | It is just for people with braces | It stops your teeth from growing
test: heard a boy at footy got his tooth knocked out and the dentist just glued it back in like nothing, what's the real lesson | Hold the tooth by the top, keep it in milk, get to a dentist fast | Throw the tooth away, it can never be saved | Wash the root with soap and dry it well | Wait a week, then take it to the dentist
test: my brother swears gum u swallow stays in ur belly 7 years, true? | Just a myth; swallowed gum passes through in a few days | True, it sticks there for exactly seven years | True, but only the minty kind of gum | False, it melts in your mouth at once
test: kids say Ruby cracked her tooth cos she's a weirdo who eats ice, should i pass it on | No, it's unkind; just learn that crunching ice can crack teeth | Yes, everyone should know she is weird | Yes, but whisper it so she cannot hear | Yes, and make fun of her new tooth too when she gets it fixed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which dental floss or an interdental brush is employed daily. | A toothbrush cannot reach the gaps between the teeth | Floss whitens the teeth more effectively than brushing | Floss replaces the need to brush altogether | Floss strengthens the roots of the teeth
exam: Whereas a permanent tooth has been dislodged by a blow, determine the procedure most consistent with the established facts. | Hold it by the white crown, keep it in milk and attend a dentist at once | Hold it by the root, scrub it clean and keep it dry | Discard it, as permanent teeth cannot be restored | Place it in the freezer and visit a dentist next week
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the appropriate everyday beverages for the protection of the teeth? | Water and milk | Fizzy drinks and fruit juice | Sweetened tea and cola | Sports drinks and squash
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a tooth that is painful upon contact with cold or sweet substances. | It may contain a cavity requiring treatment by a dentist | It is merely growing and requires no attention whatsoever from anyone | It will be cured by eating more sugar | It is a sign that brushing must cease
